A '''knowledge ecosystem''' is a network of people, organizations, tools, practices and resources through which knowledge is produced, shared, evaluated and reused. It emphasizes interactions across boundaries rather than a single organization's collection of documents.

== Ecosystem perspective ==
Knowledge ecosystem research examines interdependent actors with partially aligned interests and shared knowledge resources. Van der Borgh, Cloodt and Romme (2012) analyze value creation in a knowledge-based ecosystem through a field study. This evidence is contextual rather than a proof of a universal organizational structure. A knowledge ecosystem differs from a business ecosystem primarily in the analytical emphasis on knowledge generation and circulation, though actual systems may serve both.

== Modeling and governance ==
Represent a bounded ecosystem as a multiplex graph with actor nodes and resource nodes. Distinguish contribution, access, citation, collaboration and funding edges; absence of an observed edge is not proof of no relationship. Measure source traceability, diversity of independent contributors, reuse, correction latency and access disparities for specified tasks. Governance determines which contributions become canonical, how conflicts are resolved and how rights or responsibilities are allocated.

== Empirical design ==
Compare documented collaboration networks and research outputs before and after a change in sharing policy. Account for funding changes, field-specific publication practices and selection bias. A densely connected network may repeat errors or exclude outsiders; connectivity is not epistemic quality. An ecosystem can support collective intelligence without guaranteeing that the group performs better than its members. Reproducible evaluation requires explicit boundaries and outcome criteria.

